# axinetd rules.
#
# What answers when someone calls in. This is the replacement for ax25d, and
# it is the inbound half of making the packet network reachable from any
# language: with exec, writing a packet service becomes writing a program that
# reads standard input.
#
# Copy to /etc/axcall/inetd for everyone, or ~/.config/axcall/inetd for
# yourself; the per-user file replaces same-callsign entries in the system one.
#
# The callsign called is the whole address, so it is also the whole routing
# decision: there are no port numbers on the AX.25 side. Give each service its
# own SSID.
#
# The port column names a port from the ports file, and says which radio this
# rule is for; "-" means whichever port the instance was started on.
#
# exec runs a program with the session on its standard input and output. The
# path must be absolute. %r in an argument is replaced with the calling
# station, which is also passed as AX25_CALLER in the environment. Arguments
# are split on whitespace and there is no quoting: anything needing a space
# wants a wrapper script, which is clearer than a config line that has grown
# its own shell.
#
# The program's standard error goes to the log, not down the link.
#
# tcp hands the call to a local service instead. Bear in mind that the service
# gets a byte stream with no idea who is on the other end.
#
# axinetd will not run as root: it runs these programs on behalf of whoever
# calls in, and cannot drop privileges first. Run it as a dedicated
# unprivileged user.

# callsign    port    action
#M0LTE-1      radio   exec  /usr/local/bin/bbs --user %r
#M0LTE-2      radio   tcp   127.0.0.1:8080
#M0LTE-3      -       exec  /usr/local/bin/greet
